# Blog post structure

Mechanical conventions for every post in `src/content/posts/`. For voice and tone, see `blog-writing-conventions.mdc`. For dual-language requirements, see `blog-dual-language.mdc`.

---

## Frontmatter schema

All fields validated by `src/content.config.ts`:

```yaml
---
title: Post title in sentence case
date: YYYY-MM-DD
category: <category-id>            # one of the files in src/content/categories/
tags:                              # array of lowercase kebab-case strings
  - tag-one
  - tag-two
excerpt: One-paragraph summary in the same voice as the post body.
thumbnail: /content/posts/<slug>/<image-name>.png
lang: pt                           # PT files only; omit for EN
draft: false                       # optional; defaults to false
---
```

- `title` — sentence case, no trailing period. Subtitles allowed with an em dash: "Title — subtitle".
- `excerpt` — written in the voice of the post (not a generic AI-style summary). Used as `<meta description>` and on listings.
- `category` — required. Must match an existing file id under `src/content/categories/` (e.g. `this-blog`, `developer-tooling`, `strategy`, `ai-work`, `frontend`, `integrations`).

---

## Filename and naming

- Format: `YYYY-MM-DD-kebab-case-title.md` (or `.mdx` if the post embeds Astro components).
- Slug should be readable and stable; once published, treat the URL as permanent.
- The same slug is used for both the EN file (`src/content/posts/<slug>.md`) and the PT file (`src/content/posts/pt/<slug>.md`).

---

## Date sequencing

- Posts are listed chronologically by `date`.
- A new post's date must be **after the latest existing post**. Don't backdate.
- For a series of posts written together, use **consecutive daily dates** with the last post dated to the day of writing. This keeps the chronology readable and lets each post in the series have its own clean URL.

---

## Categories

| Category id | When to use |
|---|---|
| `this-blog` | Features, infrastructure, or meta-posts about *this specific site* |
| `developer-tooling` | Practical posts about building tools, rules, skills, agents, subagents, dev systems |
| `strategy` | Workflow philosophy, communication, leadership, planning — concept-first, tool-agnostic |
| `integrations` | Wiring third-party services and APIs (Slack, Bullhorn, Google, etc.) |
| `ai-work` | Reflective or philosophical posts on AI changing how we think and work |
| `frontend` | Frontend architecture patterns, CSS, visualizations |

Pick the category that best fits the **primary frame** of the post. If a post about a Slack-connected agent is mostly about why journaling matters, it goes in `strategy`, not `integrations`.

---

## Tags

Tags are free-form lowercase kebab-case strings. Use them generously — the search page treats them as filter chips, so more tags means better discoverability.

**Required: authorship tag.** Every post must include exactly one of:

- `human-written` — the author wrote the post entirely themselves.
- `ai-assisted` — AI helped generate parts of the content; the author directed and edited.
- `ai-generated` — substantially produced by AI.

**Topic tags** should be thorough and specific. If the post mentions a technology, tool, platform, language, framework, or AI model, tag it. Examples: `python`, `langgraph`, `fastapi`, `slack`, `postgresql`, `pydantic`, `openai`, `claude`, `gpt-4`, `astro`, `vibecoding`, `mcp`, `cursor`, `ai-agents`, `prompt-engineering`.

Aim for **6–15 relevant tags per post**. New tags can be created freely based on content. Keep them in lowercase kebab-case.

The `vibecoding` tag is reserved for practical, hands-on posts about working with AI coding agents — not for purely philosophical or strategy posts.

